  "step_2": "The individual considers the monetary difference between the two lotteries and judges it as not large enough to justify a certain loss. They express a preference for the small chance (20%) of losing nothing (i.e., 0 Euros) in Lottery A over the certainty of losing 3000 Euros in Lottery B. The justification centers on the psychological appeal of avoiding a sure loss, even if it comes with a high risk of losing more, due to the presence of a chance—however small—to lose nothing. The individual explicitly references the 'geringe Wahrscheinlichkeit, nix zu verlieren' (small probability of losing nothing) as a key factor in their decision, indicating that the possibility of a zero loss outcome is meaningful to them.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason emphasizes comparing probabilities of favorable outcomes and prefers the lottery with the higher probability of such an outcome. In this case, Lottery A has a 20% chance of a favorable outcome (0 Euros), while B has 0%, so the reason would favor A. The individual explicitly weighs the small chance of avoiding loss (i.e., the 20% probability of 0 Euros in A) against the certainty of a smaller loss in B. They value the mere presence of a chance to avoid loss, which aligns with focusing on the probability of a favorable outcome. Although the individual does not perform a full probabilistic comparison, their emphasis on the importance of having any chance (vs. none) to avoid loss indicates that they are guided by the relative probabilities of a favorable outcome. This matches the core of the decision reason. Therefore, the information considered and the justification provided are consistent with the use of the stated decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
}

